<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-22.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="003852"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003852" label="[00:38:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: I think this is your line <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/blob/4f81ae53a143821a6293fd3b0df00a52ef23f6e4/cc_core.c#L2979">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/blob/4f81ae53a143821a6293fd3b0df00a52ef23f6e4/cc_core.c#L2979</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="003856"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003856" label="[00:38:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bug on knight?</span></div><div class="line" id="003931"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003931" label="[00:39:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(% is emited there too)</span></div><div class="line" id="005735"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005735" label="[00:57:35]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, you can hack around needing autogen for later GCCs. however i found that more difficult for GCC 10 (I did attempt autogen-less); but other people were already working on guile, and it became much easier to just use guile</span></div><div class="line" id="005800"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005800" label="[00:58:00]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap currently has no notion of dependencies. so it is difficult to do any kind of reverse-optionality</span></div><div class="line" id="005934"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005934" label="[00:59:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is a bit of info in parts.rst though</span></div><div class="line" id="010006"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010006" label="[01:00:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, i mean programatically</span></div><div class="line" id="010122"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010122" label="[01:01:22]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">replacing early stuff with busybox is probably viable</span></div><div class="line" id="010129"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010129" label="[01:01:29]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i have no evidence for/against that</span></div><div class="line" id="010246"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010246" label="[01:02:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think I looked at busybox early in the live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="010251"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010251" label="[01:02:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(maybe 3 years ago)</span></div><div class="line" id="010300"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010300" label="[01:03:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it needs fairly advanced libc</span></div><div class="line" id="010304"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010304" label="[01:03:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so didn't work with meslibc</span></div><div class="line" id="010321"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010321" label="[01:03:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though meslibc now has more functions too compared to 3 years ago</span></div><div class="line" id="010341"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010341" label="[01:03:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, yeah skimming over parts.rst again actually looks much less viable</span></div><div class="line" id="010355"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010355" label="[01:03:55]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">by the time we get to musl we have most things we would want from busybox</span></div><div class="line" id="010606"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010606" label="[01:06:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;lrvick&gt; though, maybe I run m4 and company to generate the actual final compile commands each project needs &lt;-- wrt this, I don't find this to be &quot;fairly auditable&quot; at all</span></div><div class="line" id="010623"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010623" label="[01:06:23]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">something like GCC has many *thousands* of commands</span></div><div class="line" id="010844"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010844" label="[01:08:44]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so i think that you need to edit the commands run fairly significantly to make it auditable, something like what we do for early fiwix where it ends up being just cc -c -o main.o main.c over and over again</span></div><div class="line" id="010904"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010904" label="[01:09:04]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but then you run into the problem that if you ever want to change anything you have to redo all that work</span></div><div class="line" id="014510"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014510" label="[01:45:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: the rule of thumb for me is which is easier to audit. Cleaning up generated code, or reviewing all the code that does the generation</span></div><div class="line" id="014521"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014521" label="[01:45:21]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I am not actually sure which is less bad in this case</span></div><div class="line" id="014846"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014846" label="[01:48:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">related to the above, I made a very aggressive fork of live-bootstrap with the sole goal of bootstrapping an x86 gcc13 container using containerized workflows to rapidly hack on and remove steps: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/bootstrap">https://codeberg.org/stagex/bootstrap</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="015024"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015024" label="[01:50:24]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some experiments I am planning on</span></div><div class="line" id="015032"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015032" label="[01:50:32]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">replace all versions of patch, gzip, tar, sed, bzip2, bash, grep, findutils, coreutils with busybox</span></div><div class="line" id="015052"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015052" label="[01:50:52]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">replace all builds of autoconf/automake with acr</span></div><div class="line" id="015102"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015102" label="[01:51:02]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">those feel like the lowest hanging fruit</span></div><div class="line" id="015300"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015300" label="[01:53:00]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/bootstrap/src/branch/main/Containerfile#L262-L334">https://codeberg.org/stagex/bootstrap/src/branch/main/Containerfile#L262-L334</a>  is an example of IMO final build commands being easier to review than all the code used to generate them</span></div><div class="line" id="015312"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015312" label="[01:53:12]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would much rather review shell for anything nasty than c, etc</span></div><div class="line" id="015326"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015326" label="[01:53:26]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though no getting around reviewing the actual code used in each step</span></div><div class="line" id="015526"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015526" label="[01:55:26]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Also very happy to discuss/debate the merits or implausibility of any choices</span></div><div class="line" id="022247"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#022247" label="[02:22:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">shell can be quite nasty though... Generally outside bootstrapping once your script grows a bit bigger it's often better to rewrite it in some other language...</span></div><div class="line" id="022327"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022327" label="[02:23:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yes. Now that we have golang just after the first version of tinycc, that might be an unlock to remove a lot of C code</span></div><div class="line" id="022351"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022351" label="[02:23:51]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">go is not my favorite language, but it does have a huge standard library, and I would way rather review some small utilities in go than c or shell</span></div><div class="line" id="022440"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#022440" label="[02:24:40]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Wait, what? That's awesome - when and how did that happen and has someone written it up as a blogpost or something?</span></div><div class="line" id="022510"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022510" label="[02:25:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">go in stage1? It was part of a series of experiments by a couple people on the stagex team</span></div><div class="line" id="022609"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022609" label="[02:26:09]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/commit/cd2d08a01cb0470e653912bb6da5f0482df816be/packages/bootstrap/stage1/Containerfile#L208-L329">https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/commit/cd2d08a01cb0470e653912bb6da5f0482df816be/packages/bootstrap/stage1/Containerfile#L208-L329</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="022620"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022620" label="[02:26:20]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can inject it right after tcc-0.9.27 and it works</span></div><div class="line" id="022623"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022623" label="[02:26:23]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though we think we can get it earlier</span></div><div class="line" id="022741"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022741" label="[02:27:41]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but go 1.4 does require binutils, at least on a surface level</span></div><div class="line" id="022806"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#022806" label="[02:28:06]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but if we can patch that out, then we can move it to maybe step 3</span></div><div class="line" id="023206"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023206" label="[02:32:06]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but if we can replace everything in the path to binutils (automake/autoconf/coreutils/bzip2/grep/diffutils/gawk/bison) with acr/busybox then it might not be worth it</span></div><div class="line" id="032536"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#032536" label="[03:25:36]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: yeah, I probably made that bug for Knight (I will probably fix it up later this week)</span></div><div class="line" id="040230"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040230" label="[04:02:30]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: this is fair</span></div><div class="line" id="040243"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040243" label="[04:02:43]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;&lt;lrvick&gt; fossy: the rule of thumb for me is which is easier to audit. Cleaning up generated code, or reviewing all the code that does the generation&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="040657"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040657" label="[04:06:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one of my pain points with generated code beyond unauditability is its untrustworthiness; if i wanted to inject a (source code) backdoor into a well-written application, i would target committed, generated code first, where i can pass off the malicious code as committing a regenerated file (because i changed something in the inputs to said file)</span></div><div class="line" id="040718"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040718" label="[04:07:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and few people would actually review that</span></div><div class="line" id="040832"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040832" label="[04:08:32]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: would love to know how you go with busybox. did you see stikonas' comments about busybox not working with meslibc? do you have a plan to work around that, apart from attempting it?</span></div><div class="line" id="040903"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040903" label="[04:09:03]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with acr, is it actually compatible with autoconf????</span></div><div class="line" id="040910"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040910" label="[04:09:10]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or do you have to rewrite all autoconf things in acr?</span></div><div class="line" id="040934"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040934" label="[04:09:34]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">idk, acr does not look promising to me</span></div><div class="line" id="051024"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#051024" label="[05:10:24]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well on the upside, i found why gnuclasspath 0.93 was hanging: some kind of precedence wtfery <a rel="nofollow" href="http://gg6zxtreajiijztyy5g6bt5o6l3qu32nrg7eulyemlhxwwl6enk6ghad.onion/themusicgod1/classpath/commit/6cddf78d4478bdc75623bfa7af9923a3b283fbb6#diff-4a5146e6ca24c8486352bc764c6ebe95ade97a6d">http://gg6zxtreajiijztyy5g6bt5o6l3qu32nrg7eulyemlhxwwl6enk6ghad.onion/themusicgod1/classpath/commit/6cddf78d4478bdc75623bfa7af9923a3b283fbb6#diff-4a5146e6ca24c8486352bc764c6ebe95ade97a6d</a>  . now ant bootstrap build gets a little further then jamvm segfaults at  1277       table =</span></div><div class="line" id="051030"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#051030" label="[05:10:30]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(HashTable*)INST_DATA(vmdata)[ldr_data_tbl_offset]; in findHashedClass() at class.c <a rel="nofollow" href="https://shitposter.world/notice/AqKIcJdaqDygpMhJAW">https://shitposter.world/notice/AqKIcJdaqDygpMhJAW</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="051953"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051953" label="[05:19:53]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator rekado hi, check this out <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/augustss/MicroHs/tree/hugs">https://github.com/augustss/MicroHs/tree/hugs</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="052115"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052115" label="[05:21:15]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in order to run, it requires this patch to hugs that ignores integer overflow <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/augustss/hugs98-plus-Sep2006/commit/a87d3a15194e4d7724627e43a94ac1d12ab78f9c.patch">https://github.com/augustss/hugs98-plus-Sep2006/commit/a87d3a15194e4d7724627e43a94ac1d12ab78f9c.patch</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="064445"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#064445" label="[06:44:45]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ooooooooo</span></div><div class="line" id="071144"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071144" label="[07:11:44]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; fossy: acr claims to be argument compatible, so we will see. untested</span></div><div class="line" id="071155"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071155" label="[07:11:55]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Anything someone has not already tried ror proven cannot work, I might as well try</span></div><div class="line" id="071413"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071413" label="[07:14:13]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; plan B if busybox does not work out is u-root which is a busybox replacement written in go</span></div><div class="line" id="071431"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071431" label="[07:14:31]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; now that we have go very early it is maybe an option</span></div><div class="line" id="071750"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071750" label="[07:17:50]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I do see afew examples of people building busybox with tinycc though</span></div><div class="line" id="071819"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071819" label="[07:18:19]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; though I may not be able to build it against M2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="071832"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071832" label="[07:18:32]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; uclibc might be an option</span></div><div class="line" id="071849"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071849" label="[07:18:49]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; has anyone played with uclibc early?</span></div><div class="line" id="071911"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#071911" label="[07:19:11]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; tcc -&gt; uclibc -&gt; busybox is my current strawman</span></div><div class="line" id="072436"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#072436" label="[07:24:36]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; looks like busybox can also build with klibc</span></div><div class="line" id="072451"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#072451" label="[07:24:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; surely m2libc/tcc can get me one of klibc or ucllibc</span></div><div class="line" id="072456"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#072456" label="[07:24:56]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; if not musl</span></div><div class="line" id="073157"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#073157" label="[07:31:57]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; seems tinycc has supported uClibc from 0.9.25, so one would hope it can also -build- uClibc</span></div><div class="line" id="103027"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#103027" label="[10:30:27]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Yes, we looked at most libc and were only able to get musl to work</span></div><div class="line" id="141740"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#141740" label="[14:17:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">busybox can easily be compiled/linked with tinycc, although i hacked musl-libc for support with linux24</span></div><div class="line" id="141842"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#141842" label="[14:18:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i didn't track yet at which stage linux2/fiwix ABI is required, to keep a consistent bootstrapping path without circular dependency</span></div><div class="line" id="142041"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#142041" label="[14:20:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">musl-libc does need a linux2/fiwix abi of cause, mes-libc may not</span></div><div class="line" id="142314"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#142314" label="[14:23:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as far as busybox was concerned, i had chosen version 1.2.2.1 since this didn't transition to the special kbuild system as later versions did</span></div><div class="line" id="142516"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#142516" label="[14:25:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and there might have been something related to Perl needed with later versions</span></div><div class="line" id="142730"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#142730" label="[14:27:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, a busybox/tinycc bootstrapping ideally would not need a fiwix/linux2 abi</span></div><div class="line" id="143019"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#143019" label="[14:30:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's a little tricky if bootloader and kernel bootstrapping are considered; hence the proposal to re-write bootloader.S assembly parts (tccboot and kernel) for as86</span></div><div class="line" id="143304"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#143304" label="[14:33:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a kernel then can be compiled JiT with tccboot then (which i already confirmed this does work with an updated libtcc even)</span></div><div class="line" id="144606"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#144606" label="[14:46:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if a static busybox could be linked against mes-libc, this should work with a linux2/fiwix runtime abi, i think</span></div><div class="line" id="144935"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#144935" label="[14:49:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the blocker seems to be limitations of mes-libc and busybox that could not link against it (although tinycc can)</span></div><div class="line" id="145036"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145036" label="[14:50:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">something else i didn't think of, to wrap a bootable image, besides tccboot some syslinux/isolinux is needed, which too contains 16bit assembly</span></div><div class="line" id="145238"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145238" label="[14:52:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i already confirmed syslinux-3.86 compiles/links with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="145611"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145611" label="[14:56:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i do emit (non-UEFI, PC-BIOS) ISO images; with those you would either need 1) cdrecord utility to write those to cdrom</span></div><div class="line" id="145706"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145706" label="[14:57:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or 2) isohybrid/perl utility, so such an ISO image can be dumped onto usb flash</span></div><div class="line" id="145756"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145756" label="[14:57:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for 1) cdrecord i confirmed cross-compilation with tinycc already (statically linked against hacked musl-libc compatible with linux-2.4)</span></div><div class="line" id="145831"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145831" label="[14:58:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this might introduce another blocker to link cdrecord against mes-libc</span></div><div class="line" id="145901"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#145901" label="[14:59:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">2) isohybrid/perl seems not feasible with limitations of mes-libc</span></div><div class="line" id="150310"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#150310" label="[15:03:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with regards to build-time dependencies during bootstrapping, in principle, it seems all of this had to be accomplished with mes-libc and _before_ a fiwix/linux2 abi was available</span></div><div class="line" id="181950"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#181950" label="[18:19:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">seems there is no way around the intmerediate kexec towards fiwix</span></div><div class="line" id="182158"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182158" label="[18:21:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fiwix does support PATA-IDE, maybe cdrecord support could be added to live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="182402"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182402" label="[18:24:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*intermediate</span></div><div class="line" id="182635"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182635" label="[18:26:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a completely different approach which slipped through my mind: bootstrapping some FreeDOS type system as intermediate development host</span></div><div class="line" id="182736"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182736" label="[18:27:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">historically, that's how linux could be loaded (umsdos or however that was called)</span></div><div class="line" id="182839"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182839" label="[18:28:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i've not had a look at 16bit real-mode DOS for ages</span></div><div class="line" id="183055"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#183055" label="[18:30:55]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there's gotta be a gcc &lt; 15 that should be made explicit in this <a rel="nofollow" href="https://bootstrappable.org/images/jdk-bootstrap.png">https://bootstrappable.org/images/jdk-bootstrap.png</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="184446"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#184446" label="[18:44:46]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm guesisng gcc 4.7 or whatever</span></div><div class="line" id="231743"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231743" label="[23:17:43]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: at that point it is mes libc, not M2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="231746"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231746" label="[23:17:46]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but yea</span></div><div class="line" id="231801"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231801" label="[23:18:01]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: let me know how you go with those, would be interested to hear</span></div><div class="line" id="231835"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231835" label="[23:18:35]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: what do you mean by that?</span></div><div class="line" id="232000"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#232000" label="[23:20:00]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; ah yeah so the actual path would probably be more like: m2libc/M2-Planet -&gt; mes/mescc -&gt; tinycc/mescc -&gt; uClibc -&gt; busybox</span></div><div class="line" id="232441"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232441" label="[23:24:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but how do you configure uClibc build system?</span></div><div class="line" id="232500"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232500" label="[23:25:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">don't you need to run &quot;make config&quot; or something like that</span></div><div class="line" id="232516"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232516" label="[23:25:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which depends on bison and lex</span></div><div class="line" id="232615"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232615" label="[23:26:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and posibly SHELL as well <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/kraj/uClibc/blob/ca1c74d67dd115d059a875150e10b8560a9c35a8/Makefile">https://github.com/kraj/uClibc/blob/ca1c74d67dd115d059a875150e10b8560a9c35a8/Makefile</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="232813"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232813" label="[23:28:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think we did look at all possible libc's...</span></div><div class="line" id="232827"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232827" label="[23:28:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and musl was the only option that we were able to proceed with</span></div><div class="line" id="232909"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232909" label="[23:29:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I guess we do build bash before musl too</span></div><div class="line" id="232914"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232914" label="[23:29:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so maybe shell is not super bad</span></div><div class="line" id="232927"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232927" label="[23:29:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but then you need to build bash before busybox too</span></div><div class="line" id="232951"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#232951" label="[23:29:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and bash will automatically pull in all those earlier tools between tcc and musl</span></div><div class="line" id="233009"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#233009" label="[23:30:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps you could avoid bash with gash...</span></div><div class="line" id="233018"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#233018" label="[23:30:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that will be slow...</span></div><div class="line" id="235124"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235124" label="[23:51:24]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; for uclibc the only way forward I could think of really early is just running hacking up the makefiles and make config to produce the final set of actual tcc commands that would be run as a kaem script.</span></div><div class="line" id="235133"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235133" label="[23:51:33]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; but is that 100 commands that could be easily dealt with, or thousands, I don't actaully know</span></div><div class="line" id="235317"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235317" label="[23:53:17]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; but probably not low hanging fruit I grant</span></div><div class="line" id="235339"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235339" label="[23:53:39]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; the easiest wins then may be to build busybox just after the first musl when we build go, then can drop a lot of builds after that point</span></div><div class="line" id="235450"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235450" label="[23:54:50]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; It is a hard puzzle I know you guys spent a lot of time on. I appreciate you talking me out of known dead ends so I can focus my attention on paths not fully explored yet.</span></div><div class="line" id="235852"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#235852" label="[23:58:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what would the benefit of uclibc be, if musl-libc can be hacked for linux2.4 api/abi?</span></div><div class="line" id="235958"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235958" label="[23:59:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; our use case is not to build a linux kernel</span></div><br /></div></body></html>